  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is an energy centre?",
      "a": "It is the building that makes the heat for a district heating network. It might use waste heat from a power station, factory or incinerator, large heat pumps drawing on rivers or sewers, or efficient boilers.",
      "asks": [
        "Where does district heat come from?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do district heating pipes stay hot?",
      "a": "The steel or plastic pipes are wrapped in thick foam insulation inside a protective outer case, so hot water can travel kilometres while losing only a few degrees.",
      "asks": [
        "Do heat network pipes lose heat?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do heat networks have big hot water tanks?",
      "a": "The tanks store heat made when it is cheap or plentiful and release it at busy times, like cold mornings, so the energy centre does not have to be sized for the biggest peak.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a thermal store?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Do you still have a boiler with district heating?",
      "a": "No. Each building has a heat interface unit, a small box with a heat exchanger, controls and a meter, which takes the place of a boiler.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a heat interface unit?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Which country uses district heating the most?",
      "a": "Denmark is a leader: around two thirds of Danish homes get their heat this way, and Copenhagen's network heats almost the whole city. Iceland heats most homes with geothermal hot water.",
      "asks": [
        "Why is district heating common in Denmark?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Around two thirds of homes in Denmark are heated by district heating.",
    "Copenhagen's district heating network supplies about 98 percent of the city's heat demand."
  ],
